

⭐ CMB ORGANISM RENDERING (Final Form)

Organisms are rendered as dynamic visual entities layered on top of the cinematic viewport.Each organism has:

a shape

a boundary

a state

a lifecycle

a motion path

The viewer renders these using simple, predictable rules.

⭐ 1. Organism Types → Rendering Styles

Each organism type maps to a visual identity.

warp_bubble      → soft glowing boundary
time_crystal     → pulsing geometric core
frozen_pocket    → cold blue contour
shear_node       → sharp angular outline

This is type → style, nothing more.

The viewer doesn’t simulate organisms — it renders them.

⭐ 2. Core Rendering Elements

Every organism uses the same four visual components:

A. Boundary

A line or contour around the organism.

color = type‑based

thickness = stage‑based

opacity = energy‑based

B. Fill (optional)

A soft interior glow or heatmap.

warp bubbles → purple/blue

frozen pockets → cyan

time crystals → gold

C. Label

A small HUD tag:

O1  warp_bubble  growth

D. Trail

A motion path showing where the organism has moved.

fade over time

color = organism type

length = configurable

These four elements create the organism’s visual identity.

⭐ 3. Lifecycle Rendering

Organisms change appearance based on their stage.

spawn     → small, faint boundary
growth    → expanding radius, brighter glow
stable    → steady boundary + label
decay     → flicker + shrinking radius
death     → fade out

The viewer reads "stage" from the data file and adjusts rendering accordingly.

No simulation.Just visual state mapping.

⭐ 4. Energy‑Based Rendering

Organisms have an "energy" value (0–1).This drives:

glow intensity

boundary brightness

trail opacity

pulse frequency

Example:

energy = 0.9 → bright, fast pulse
energy = 0.2 → dim, slow pulse

This makes organisms feel alive.

⭐ 5. Geometry Rendering

Organisms have:

"center": [x, y]

"radius": r

The viewer draws:

a circle (default)

or a shape defined by "type"

Warp bubbles → circlesTime crystals → polygonsFrozen pockets → irregular contours

This is shape mapping, not physics.

⭐ 6. Motion Rendering

The viewer tracks the organism’s center across frames:

frame[n].organisms[i].center
frame[n+1].organisms[i].center

This produces:

trails

velocity vectors

motion smoothing

This gives organisms presence in the time fabric.

⭐ 7. Interaction

Clicking an organism shows:

ID

type

stage

energy

radius

center

blueprint origin

lifecycle timeline

This is the organism inspector.

⭐ 8. Rendering Pipeline (Final Form)

for each organism:
    draw boundary
    draw fill (if enabled)
    draw trail
    draw label

That’s it.

Simple.Predictable.Fast.GPU‑friendly.



